1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,280
We are now at the exact
point where the sea meets
the river, one of the most
significant moments on the
entire route through the
Grotta del Bue Marino.
If you look down beneath

2
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:22,689
you, you will notice a small
difference in level:
a natural rocky step just
a few centimetres high, but
enough to separate two
different worlds. In the
lower level is salt water,

3
00:00:22,689 --> 00:00:34,098
coming from the sea. In the
slightly higher level
is fresh water, fed by the
underground river. During
the summer months, when
rainfall is scarce, the
river significantly reduces

4
00:00:34,098 --> 00:00:44,609
its flow and tends
to stagnate. In these
conditions the two bodies
of water remain separate,
maintaining an almost
motionless equilibrium.
During the winter months,

5
00:00:44,609 --> 00:00:56,338
on the other hand, the river
is fed once again by rain.
The flow increases and,
already in the low season,
if you are lucky, it
is right here at this spot
that you may observe a small

6
00:00:56,338 --> 00:01:08,259
waterfall of fresh water
pouring down towards the sea
level. It is a particularly
evocative moment: before our
very eyes, the process that
over millennia has
contributed to the formation

7
00:01:08,259 --> 00:01:19,604
of these cavities is made
clearly visible. Here we are
not merely witnessing the
meeting of two bodies
of water, but the meeting
of two forces: the marine
and the fluvial. It is

8
00:01:19,604 --> 00:01:28,000
a dynamic equilibrium, which
varies with the seasons and
which continues, even today,
to slowly shape the interior
of the mountain.
